In the `reserve_new` method of `TreeRangeAllocator`, the oneway spam detection (calling `self.low_oneway_space(pid)`) occurs before the new buffer is inserted into the tree. Specifically, the detection is performed at line:

rust
let oneway_spam_detected =
    is_oneway && new_oneway_space < self.size / 10 && self.low_oneway_space(pid);


The new buffer is only inserted later via `self.tree.insert(tree_node)` (around line 210). Consequently, `low_oneway_space` does not count the new buffer, allowing large oneway transactions to bypass spam detection. This matches the root cause description: 'in TreeRange the detection ran before inserting the current request, so the new buffer wasn't counted.' No fix is present in this initial commit.
